## Package comparison

The phase package remains aligned with approved Phase 1. This rework materially improves retry and shutdown coverage, but concurrency correctness and required ownership boundaries are still incomplete.

## Findings

### High — Authority validation and state publication are still racy

`coordinator.ts:153-160` checks the sampling generation and `authority`, then calls `accept` in a later effect. A provider snapshot or successful command can increment authority after that check but before `accept`, allowing the older sample to publish over newer authority. In addition, `accept` itself reads the current revision, updates authority, and writes state in separate operations (`:115-128`), so concurrent command/snapshot accepts can both derive the same revision and lose a monotonic update. The package requires pre-snapshot/pre-command samples never to regain authority and one monotonic revision authority; those checks and publication need one synchronization boundary.

### High — Sampling completion can still produce more than one catch-up sample

The two individual `Ref.modify` calls remove the original lost-update race, but `coordinator.ts:172-176` marks the lane inactive before recursively claiming the follow-up. A fresh trigger can claim the lane in that gap; the recursive call then marks that new sample stale and pending, causing another sample afterward. Triggers overlapping completion therefore need not coalesce into exactly one follow-up. The test covers a burst while the provider Promise is blocked, not this completion-boundary interleaving.

### High — Multiple snapshots corrupt retry progression

`attemptRetrySchedule` consumes only one reset token with `Queue.poll` (`provider.ts:79-88`). A normal attempt can emit many valid snapshots before terminating. Extra tokens remain queued and reset later failed attempts even when those attempts emitted no snapshot, keeping their delays at one second instead of progressing through 1/2/4/8. The new test enqueues only one snapshot and cannot detect this. Reset processing must drain/coalesce all snapshots belonging to the completed attempt without blocking schedule evaluation.

### High — The provider event bridge is unbounded and the required Effect test Layer is still absent

`eventsFromAttemptAdapter` bridges callbacks into three `Queue.unbounded` queues (`provider.ts:100-107`), contrary to the package's bounded-queue/Effect-owned callback boundary. A fast or late source can therefore grow event/reset queues without a bound. Separately, `createFakeProvider` remains a Promise/callback `LegacySessionProvider` wrapped by `layerFromLegacy`, rather than the required Effect-native deterministic test Layer. This leaves several explicit acceptance cases unproved, including navigation timing, all optimistic projections, blocked reconciliation/late transport completion, and instrumented exact-once connection-forwarder finalization. The passing verification transcript does not close those package requirements.
